  "text": "Allophone: A variant form of a phoneme that occurs in specific phonetic environments.\n\nArticulatory Phonetics: The study of how speech sounds are produced through the movement of speech organs.\n\nAssimilation: A phonological process where a sound becomes more like a neighboring sound.\n\nBilingualism: The ability to speak and understand two languages.\n\nConsonant: A speech sound produced with some obstruction of airflow in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant Cluster: A group of consonants occurring together in a word without any vowels.\n\nDialect: A regional or social variety of a language distinguished by vocabulary, pronunciation, and grammar.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound in which the vocal tract is tuned to two different vowels in rapid succession.\n\nDissimilation: A phonological process where a sound becomes less like a neighboring sound.\n\nEjective Consonant: A consonant produced with a burst of air from the lungs, often found in certain languages.\n\nFricative: A consonant sound made by narrowing the vocal tract, causing audible friction.\n\nGlottal Stop: A speech sound made by closing the vocal folds briefly, then releasing them.\n\nIdiophone: A term used in phonology to refer to a phoneme that has no specific phonetic realization.\n\nInterlanguage: The language system developed by second language learners, which may differ from native and target languages.\n\nLactic: A phonological term referring to a sound made with the dental or alveolar region.\n\nLateral Consonant: A consonant sound made by directing air around the sides of the tongue.\n\nLexical Stress: The emphasis placed on certain syllables within a word.\n\nLingua Franca: A language used for communication between speakers of different native languages.\n\nMorpheme: The smallest meaningful unit in a language, such as a prefix, root, or suffix.\n\nNasal Consonant: A consonant sound produced with the soft palate lowered, allowing air to escape through the nose.\n\nNasalization: The process of nasalizing vowels when a nasal consonant is adjacent.\n\nObstruent: A consonant produced with a significant blockage or friction of airflow.\n\nOvershadowing: A phonological process where a later sound suppresses or alters an earlier one.\n\nPalatal Consonant: A consonant produced with the tongue raised toward the hard palate.\n\nParalinguistic: Non-linguistic vocal features that accompany speech, such as tone or pitch.\n\nPhoneme: The smallest unit of sound in a language that can distinguish meaning.\n\nPhonetics: The study of the physical properties of speech sounds.\n\nPhonology: The study of the sound system of a language.\n\nPrefix: A morpheme added to the beginning of a word to modify its meaning.\n\nProsody: The study of the rhythmic, stress, and intonation patterns in speech.\n\nProto-language: A hypothetical ancestor language from which modern languages are derived.\n\nRhotic: A term used to describe the pronunciation of the 'r' sound in certain languages.\n\nSibilant: A type of fricative consonant involving the tongue and the roof of the mouth.\n\nSyllable: A unit of speech that consists of one vowel sound with possibly surrounding consonants.\n\nVowel: A speech sound produced without obstruction of airflow in the vocal tract.\n\nVowel Harmony: A phonological process where vowels in a word are harmonized to be similar in quality.\n\nVowel Length: The difference in sound duration between long and short vowels in a language.\n\nWord Stress: The emphasis placed on a particular syllable within a word.",
